{
	"$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
	// THE STATEMENTS SITE (03 Sep 2026): a second Worker, on its own address, holding nothing but
	// ciphertext. See stmt/worker.js for why it is not a route on salt-command. Deploy and address
	// it with `-c wrangler.stmt.jsonc` on every wrangler command; the default config is the desk's.
	//
	// THE NAME IS THE ADDRESS, and it is cryptic on purpose (his instruction): a customer scans a
	// code that opens https://<name>.<account>.workers.dev, and nothing in that name says salt,
	// statement or ledger. Change it here and rebuild the statements, because the QR carries it
	// (SALT_BASE_URL in tools/make_statements.mjs must agree). A custom domain on this Worker is
	// the stronger version of the same idea, and goes in a "routes" block here when there is one.
	"name": "k7m3p2",
	"main": "stmt/worker.js",
	// COMPATIBILITY DATE, raised from 2026-08-08 on his instruction of 22 Sep 2026. Checked
	// before moving it: not one JavaScript flag defaults on between those two dates, the only
	// entry in the window being python_workers_314 on 2026-09-08, so this changes no
	// behaviour today. It is the newest date this workerd (1.20260921.1) supports, so local
	// dev and the edge agree. rev.json's id does not cover this file, so a change here is
	// deployed BY HAND.
	"compatibility_date": "2026-09-21",
	// ITS OWN STORE. One key per username (u:<username>) holding that customer's envelope and
	// verifier, plus the attempt counters (fail:) and the opened marks (seen:). Nothing from the
	// desk's namespace is bound here, so this Worker cannot read the queue or the vault even by
	// a bug. Create with:
	//   npx wrangler kv namespace create stmt -c wrangler.stmt.jsonc
	// then paste the printed id below in place of the placeholder. The deploy job stands down
	// while the placeholder is still here, so an unfinished setup cannot break a ledger deploy.
	"kv_namespaces":
	[
		{
			"binding": "STMT",
			"id": "d193671c174545c29dbef5604b1d9502",
			"remote": true
		}
	],
	// THE ORDER BOOK (S10, D10, his answer of 24 Sep 2026): ONE Durable Object for the whole site's
	// orders, stmt/orderbook.js, SQLite-backed (the kind the free plan offers). The migration makes the
	// class on the first deploy of this file; a deploy is how it reaches production, and the push job
	// deploys this site on every run that deploys. Never rename the class or re-tag the migration: a
	// tag already applied is a record, and a new class is a new tag after it.
	"durable_objects": {
		"bindings": [
			{ "name": "ORDERBOOK", "class_name": "OrderBook" }
		]
	},
	"migrations": [
		{ "tag": "v1", "new_sqlite_classes": ["OrderBook"] }
	],
	// The owner's override lives in the STMT_MASTER secret:
	//   npx wrangler secret put STMT_MASTER -c wrangler.stmt.jsonc
	// Unset, the override is simply off and every open needs the customer's own password.
	//
	// TWO MORE SECRETS SINCE 06 Sep 2026, both set by `node tools/stmt-setup.mjs`:
	//   STMT_DESK_KEY           the shared key the desk's Worker sends on /desk/orders; unset,
	//                           those routes refuse everything
	//   STMT_VAPID_PRIVATE_JWK  the site's own Web Push signing key, for waking a customer's
	//                           phone when his order moves; the public half is the var below
	// THE OWNER'S ROUTE, /all, IS BEHIND CLOUDFLARE ACCESS (10 Sep 2026). These two name the
	// application stmt/access.js verifies the token against, and they are not secrets: the audience
	// tag is public, and holding it opens nothing. WITH EITHER EMPTY THE ROUTE ANSWERS 401 AND HANDS
	// OVER NOTHING, which is why it is safe to deploy this before the application exists.
	//   ACCESS_TEAM  the team name in <team>.cloudflareaccess.com, "maakmal" since 11 Aug 2026
	//   ACCESS_AUD   the application's Application Audience tag, from the Access application on
	//                <this worker>.workers.dev/all. Paste it here and deploy; there is no secret
	//                to set. The QR Command admin application is the working example of the shape.
	"vars": {
		// application "Salt statements owner", 67280e0b-7a34-4789-ad63-697e292b7844,
		// created 10 Sep 2026 on his instruction: self-hosted, one-time PIN, one Allow
		// policy on his address, 24h session. Deleting it does not open /all: with this
		// value present and the application gone, no token verifies and the route is 401.
		"ACCESS_TEAM": "maakmal",
		"ACCESS_AUD": "14a921e2b5c1ba14ca75026b930208093644ef349993a4fc66015f689060fecf",
		"STMT_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY": "BDIvD4Y6s-Ucr6twgOkmcYPZiUhGLaXMNzAqFrBKhdNCNy-Iyr9xYl7iWLTEAfL1rtQOJJg7g-m8ZeokZqM493c",
		"STMT_VAPID_SUBJECT": "mailto:maakmal97@icloud.com",
		// S10 (D10): THE SWITCH for the site's orders (stmt/orders.js, onBook). "object+kv" is the week of
		// reading both: the orders in the one Durable Object, every order it changes written to its KV key
		// behind it, KV read when the object cannot answer, and an hourly check kept at KV orderbook:check.
		// "kv" is the way back. "object" is 10.5, after seven days of that check clean (cleanSince); it
		// stops the KV writes, and deleting the old keys is a fold of its own.
		"ORDER_STORE": "object+kv",
		// The move-in's generation: a one-minute freeze and a copy of every KV order, once per value. Raise
		// it by one only to come back from "kv", when KV has taken moves the object has not.
		"ORDER_MOVE_IN": "1"
	},
	// THE HOURLY CHASE (v700, his instruction of 18 Sep 2026): "the customer will be notified every
	// hour to pay if it is an advanced order", day and night, until it is paid. This Worker had no
	// clock at all until now; it woke a phone only as a side effect of the desk touching an order.
	// On the hour, every hour, and stmt/worker.js's scheduled() caps it at one wake an hour per
	// customer through the chased:<username> mark, so a tick that fires twice cannot chase twice.
	//
	// A CRON HERE REACHES PRODUCTION ONLY THROUGH A DEPLOY OF THIS CONFIG, and public/rev.json's id
	// does not cover this file, so tools/update.mjs will report the phone current and deploy nothing.
	// Deploy it by hand: npx wrangler deploy -c wrangler.stmt.jsonc
	"triggers": {
		"crons": ["0 * * * *"]
	},
	"observability": {
		"enabled": true
	}
}
